  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1118 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nGOES water vapor imagery currently shows a bowling ball \nlow off the coast of CA. Moisture in the leading edge of the \nassociated trough can be seen traveling up into the Four Corners \nRegion. Also, on radar scattered thunderstorms can be see seen \nover parts of AZ into NM. This moisture and the leading of the \ntrough is expected to creep into the far western Panhandles Friday\nnight with shower and thunderstorm activity holding short in NM. \nPoPs remain in the western combined Panhandles mainly near the \nstate line through much of Saturday before expanding across the \nentirety of the combined Panhandles Sat night into Sun. The NBM \ndoes give PoPs as high as 90 across the central to western parts \nof Panhandles during the day on Sun. With PWATs near 1\" for this \ntime of year, and the expected speed of the system, there is a \npotential for multiple rounds of efficient rain producers Sat into\nMon. By the end of Sun night NBM probs do give widespread 40% \nplus for 1\" of rain across the combined Panhandles. Both the LREF \nand NBM percentiles are pinging an area around Dallam and Hartley\nCounty for the highest storm total rain. The 90th percentile is \nright around 4\" of rain for this area. \n\nAll the expected rain activity going into Sun should drop \nafternoon highs even more into the 60s for most of the Panhandles\nfor Sun afternoon. Depending on the speed of the system the far \neastern parts of the combined Panhandles may make it to the lower \n70s still. PoPs do linger into Monday with the eastern counties in\nthe FA hanging on to a 60% chance for rain. Monday night, is when\nPoPs begin to really fall off. A frontal boundary is progged to \nshift winds to the north late Mon into Mon night. A bit of CAA \nwill help reinforce some cooler air leading to widespread 60 \ndegree temps in the afternoon. Potentially some upper 50s in the \nnorthwestern Panhandles. Lower 60s daytime highs expected to last \ninto Tue with temperatures rebounding to the upper 60s for Wed.",
